writeMd("miller-north-three-province-comparison-2026-09-06.md", `# Miller North three-province comparison\n\n> Descriptive comparison of accountability structures, not harm severity.\n\n| Dimension | British Columbia | Saskatchewan | Alberta |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| How concern became public | Independent systemic review commissioned amid widespread reports | Anonymized cohort accounts examined by a commissioned external review | Advisory-panel process plus earlier public engagement evidence |\n| Main accountability form | 24-recommendation review and task-team implementation reporting | Ten Calls to Action, apology, program response and litigation | 22 advisory recommendations and a government implementation plan |\n| Government role | Lead, legislator, funder and reporter | Health-system response plus later federal committee/law context | Panel sponsor, response author, funder and program administrator |\n| Indigenous organization role | Governance partner, standards co-author and evaluation source | Review authorship, advocacy and recommended equal governance partnership | Panel leadership, design partner and funded recipient/service roles |\n| Regulator/law role | Professional-regulation reform and PIDA coverage | Procedural litigation and later federal Criminal Code amendment | Health Charter review context; advocate has non-court, non-regulator limits |\n| Implementation evidence | Broadest, but distributed across institutions | Strong for one birth-support service; weakly disaggregated for the full ten Calls | Strong for implementation machinery; incomplete 22-row reporting |\n| Public reporting gap | No current comprehensive 24-row ledger located | No current comprehensive ten-action ledger located | No current comprehensive 22-row ledger located |\n| Funding boundary | Program-level context not attributed to individual recommendations | Operational program found without a service-level amount | Program and recipient funding found; not equivalent to outcomes |\n\n## Shared finding\n\nAll three cases benefit from the same evidence path—problem or advice → recommendation → response → implementation mechanism → later evidence—but the entry point and authority differ. B.C. is review-and-report led; Saskatchewan combines cohort review, service response and court process; Alberta is advisory-panel and administrative-implementation led.\n\n## Recurrent gap\n\nRecommendation-level public reporting becomes less complete over time. That is a source-coverage finding, not evidence that institutions did not act.\n`)

writeMd("miller-north-research-policy-product-specification-2026-09-06.md", `# Miller North Research & Policy product specification\n\n## Product decision\n\nUse one private case projection over existing domain records. Do not create a parallel public database.\n\n## Shared components\n\n1. **Incident or cohort overview** — plain-language subject identity, geography and evidence boundary.\n2. **Owner-first synthesis** — one takeaway, three review-first items, five review-next items and a single next move.\n3. **Accountability timeline** — dated events with source references.\n4. **Evidence path** — source-bearing edges only.\n5. **Recommendation tracker** — optional row-level status with a visible non-effectiveness notice.\n6. **Government / policy / law** — context scope shown as direct, response, governance or territorial only.\n7. **Implementation depth** — acknowledgement, commitment, funding, policy, creation, operation, reporting, evaluation and outcome remain distinct.\n8. **Knowledge gaps** — maximum two next questions plus a stopping reason for any follow-up.\n9. **Source trail** — authoritative family and role on each source.\n\n## Case-specific components\n\n- B.C.: 24-row thematic recommendation tracker and source-numbering discrepancy notice.\n- Saskatchewan: cohort/anonymity banner, court-procedure boundary and later-law context.\n- Alberta: candidate-selection rationale, panel recommendation crosswalk and program/advocate mandate boundaries.\n\n## Private preview layout\n\nCase selector → What happened → Why it matters → Timeline → Accountability → Government/policy/law → Implementation → What remains unknown. The preview must have no write, approval or publication controls.\n`)

